A||N||15||DiggityDan||Rearend likes to shimmy????||||1||
Z||000000||DiggityDan||04-01-2000||01:55 AM||||I just bought my 85 GT and I love the car. I have noticed a very drastic shimmy in the rear-end. Usually over bumps and dips. The car squats and does a little fish tail. I plan on redoing the front and rear-end complety but for now, I would just like to do enough to get this taken care of so I can safely drive the car. Any help would be greatly appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||04-01-2000||02:12 AM||||First check to make sure the same size tires on on both sides of the car. Check air pressure of tires. You could also have bad struts. These are the easy fixes after that it's bushings and ball joints.||||reg||1
Z||000002||SCCA FIERO||04-01-2000||02:14 AM||||Welcome to the wonderful world of bump-steer, it plauges the 84-87 Fieros. You can fix it with a few bucks with a kit from RCC(does it work batboy?) or you can fix it with alot of bucks with a kit from Ryane. Could also be the tierods or worn bushings as well as a low tire... the list can get pretty big. This is the reason I got an 88.||||reg||1
Z||000003||DiggityDan||04-01-2000||02:36 AM||||ok...explain bump-steer....<BR>where do I get information on the kits? web address? Struts sound possible. I had the cradle out when I changed the clutch and I checked the ball joints and tie rods and they appeared ok, I could have overlooked something, though. Tires are same size and specified pressure. Thanks for all the ideas. One more, question.....what's the best strut to use on these cars for fairly aggressive handling?||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||04-01-2000||02:53 AM||||When the tire hits a bump, because of the location of the tierod, it deflects some and this changes the geometery of the tire. When you hit a bum the tire and control arm travel upwards (obviousily) and the tierod angle changes and the distance gets longer. This changes the toe of the rear tire and causes a moment of "bump-steer".<P>Here is the Ryane link  <A HREF="http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/</A> <P>Not sure of the RCC site.||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||04-01-2000||05:22 AM||||Also check to make sure that your alignment is right!||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||04-01-2000||08:46 AM||||Sounds like struts and after you replace struts, you have to get a 4 wheel alignment.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DiggityDan||04-01-2000||01:20 PM||||I would like information on the RCC kit....namely, how do I get ahold of them.<BR>Another question. If 88's do not suffer from this problem, can I implant 88 suspension parts into my 85. Well....I'm off to the parts house to get some struts.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||04-01-2000||02:36 PM||||You'll need to rent or borrow a spring compressor. Be careful, those things can hurt if they get away from you. It's easier to remove the spring and strut as one unit, then switch the spring over to the new strut. Then put the whole strut and spring unit back onto the car. It will now be completely out of alignment, so be careful. You'll ruin a tire or two in no time. Get it aligned ASAP.<P>RCC telephone: 817-536-3820<BR>e-mail: dwayne9@airmail.net<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 04-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||04-01-2000||04:57 PM||||when replacing struts on something this old check over the top bearing carefully. consider getting a boot kit for the strut. the boots protect the rod and they look nice.||||reg||1
Z||000010||lowCG||04-01-2000||05:23 PM||||And while you're at it,check the bushings in the control arms,and the front balljoints,since you never know when they've last been serviced.<BR>Just start by voilently pulling on the tops of all the tires while the car is sitting on the ground,then when you hear something strange,or feel some extra movement,have a friend do the pulling while you try and locate the source from underneath.||||reg||1
Z||000011||N_Man||04-01-2000||06:12 PM||||My '87GT's front end kind of rocks back and forth when i hit a bump at speeds over about 65 mph. Its pretty unnerving because it causes the car to wander back and forth a noticable amount.<P>I was wondering if this would be caused by 'bumpsteer' or if I might have a different problem.||||reg||5
Z||000012||DiggityDan||04-01-2000||08:06 PM||||I hate living in a small town...parts stores don't carry any parts, order only. So I decided to do more checking on the suspension. I have found absolutely no play in the back end. I guess this is that bump-steer thing, coupled with bad struts. As a side not, I found that my rack-and-pinion is trashed, lots of slack in the unit. I have a fealing this is exagerating the problem I am having. <P>Anybody know where the best place to get a good rack. I have called around and most people only show one rack for 85, although one company showed two, a sport and regular. The sport having 3 turns to lock and the regular having 3.5. Which this doesn't make sense since mine is less than 2.5...go figure. Any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000013||theogre||04-02-2000||12:35 AM||||I have the sport rack. 3 turns lock to lock.<P>Since you have to order it anyway. Try Wrenchead and Carparts. They have damn near every part Fiero needs minus body and interior. Use MOOG suspension parts. KYB for struts and shock is very competivly priced with Monroe and other better grade units. (I'm doing KYB on my SC.)<P>Cradle bushings could also be trashed. that will let the rear do anoying things. Get new pollyurethane ones at <A HREF="http://www.suspension.com" TARGET=_blank>www.suspension.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||04-02-2000||09:29 AM||||Dan, I was wondering how you were checking the steering rack. It's possible the inner tie rods are actually what's worn out. My experience has been that these are high wear items on 100,000+ mileage Fieros (along with front lower ball joints and wheel bearings). About the only thing that wears on the rack is the passenger-side bushing where the inner tie rod goes in. See if you can wiggle this back and forth (front of car to back of car). If all the play is side to side (in and out of the rack) it's probably tie rods. Hope this helps, if not you maybe others.||||reg||1
Z||000015||DiggityDan||04-02-2000||12:42 PM||||Well...first let me say, I don't have an inner tie rod....its more like a ball and socket type configuration. What I did first was jack up car, lock steering wheel, grabbed wheels at 3 & 9 and shook. lots of slack on both sides. after further inspection, discovered it was in that area, under a boot. Removed boot, got under car with someone shacking wheel and saw the assembly inside the rack moving up and down. <P>I am baffled at the fact that I don't have an inner tie rod. If anyone knows why, let me know. Is this the sport rack or the regular rack. My car is an 85 GT, so I assumed it was the sport rack.||||reg||1
